# Every text file is LF — in the index and in the working tree.
#
# `text=auto` lets git detect text from binary; `eol=lf` overrides a checkout
# that would otherwise follow `core.autocrlf`, which is `true` on the machine
# this is developed on. Without the `eol=lf` half, a file's working-tree bytes
# differ from its committed blob while `git diff` normalises the difference away
# and reports nothing — so a tool that hashes or byte-compares a file disagrees
# with version control and cannot be told why.
#
# That cost real time twice, in both directions:
#
#   * `zig fmt --check` reported every file in the repo as unformatted on a
#     Windows checkout, burying the ones that genuinely were. This is what the
#     original `*.zig`/`*.zon` rules below were added for.
#   * `tools/mutate.py` restored source with `Path.write_text`, which rewrote LF
#     as CRLF. `cmd_job.zig`'s adjacency gate reads its own source and ends a
#     function body at "\n}\n", a string CRLF text cannot contain — so that gate
#     failed for mutations in unrelated files and contaminated a whole
#     26-mutation pass. The runner was not silent, it was misattributing.
#   * The byte sizes quoted for the two evidence artefacts were measured on CRLF
#     working-tree copies, overstating the committed blobs by exactly one byte
#     per line.
#
# All 137 tracked files are text today, so this rule covers every one of them.
# The `binary` marks below are for file types that do not exist here yet: an exe
# or an image added later must not be silently line-ending-converted, and saying
# so now is cheaper than diagnosing it then.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.so binary
*.dylib binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.tgz binary
*.db binary

# Redundant against the catch-all, kept because these are the two that broke
# first and an explicit rule is what a reader looks for.
*.zig text eol=lf
*.zon text eol=lf
